<!--
    Objective-C reference slots and selector stubs (`option objc on|off`,
    `option objcrefconst on|off`).

    Issue: on an iOS app (Toyota.app, 84 MB arm64, Swift + ObjC) every message
    send read `objc_msgSendSuper2(&super, dat_1045b76f0)` even with `objc on`,
    and 274 of 1,622 calls in a 500-function sample were `sub_1034xxxxx(obj)`:
    the `__objc_selrefs` slot the selector is loaded from had no name, and the
    `__objc_stubs` entry a modern link routes most sends through (`adrp/ldr x1`
    of the selref, `br objc_msgSend`) was an anonymous function.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 external-refinement. `analyzers/objc/kuna_objcrefs.rs`
    names each `__objc_selrefs` slot `selRef_<sel>`, each `__objc_classrefs` /
    `__objc_superrefs` slot `classRef_<Class>` / `superRef_<Class>` (a chained
    bind slot from its import name), and each `__objc_stubs` entry
    `objc_msgSend$<sel>` with the prototype of the image's own method for that
    selector pinned past the `x1` the stub owns. `objcrefconst` reports the
    rebase slots constant, the `dynrelocs` RELRO mechanism, so a load through
    one folds to the selector string / class symbol (Ghidra's
    ObjectiveC2_ClassAnalyzer marks the same three blocks read-only). The loader
    unpacks the LC_DYLD_CHAINED_FIXUPS rebases first (`loader/kuna_dynrelocs.rs (macho_chained)`,
    under `dynrelocs`), without which a chained slot holds a packed chain word.

    P0 assertions: `option objc on|off`, `option objcrefconst on|off`.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture; every pass clears it
    and loads the real arm64 fixture through the object bootstrap. The fixture
    is `macho_objc_refs.m` (two root classes, `Derived : Base`, an undefined
    `Remote` class resolved by dynamic lookup) linked `-fixup_chains
    -objc_stubs_fast -x`; see the fixtures README for the stub recipe.

    Pass 1 (`option objc off`, the shipped default) reproduces the BUG: the
    super send reads its selector from `dat_1000081f8` and its class from
    `dat_1000081f0`, `_use` calls the anonymous stub `sub_1000008c0`, and
    `_remote` sends to `dat_100008230`.
    Pass 2 (`option objc on`, `option objcrefconst off`) proves the NAMING on
    its own: `selRef_greet_`, `superRef_Derived`, `classRef_Remote` (a bind),
    and the stub call `objc_msgSend$count(a0)`; `_use2` passes `5` to
    `objc_msgSend$greet:` with no dead `x1` argument.
    Pass 3 (`option objc on`, `objcrefconst` at its default) is the FIX: the
    same loads fold, so the super send reads `sel_greet_` with
    `_OBJC_CLASS_$_Derived` in the super struct and `_tag` sends to
    `_OBJC_CLASS_$_Derived`; the bind slot stays `classRef_Remote`.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c0035fd6
</bytechunk>
